The equilibrium constant of a reaction is related to the thermodynamic characteristics of the reaction. This means it depends on the difference in energy between reactants and products and not on the rate of conversion between reactants and products.
The equilibrium constant of a reaction does not depend on the initial concentration or pressure of the species involved. However, $K$ deppends on temperature and the change deppends at the same time on the energetic character of the reaction. Exothermic reaction, reactions producing heat, are favoured at low temperature, whereas endothermic reactions, reactions consuming heat, are favoured at high temperature. This section qualitatively addresses the change of $K_p$ with temperature, introducing the van't Hoff equation.